1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
You know the Giza pyramids. But almost nobody knows why Egypt never died after it lost its own kings for 2,300 years.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Persians, Greeks, Romans, Arabs, Turks, the British. Every one of those empires ruled Egypt, and every one of them is gone. Egypt is still here. The answer is in this picture.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
By the end of this video, you'll know exactly how it worked. That line is the Nile. Nearly everyone in Egypt lives along it, because almost everything else is desert.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Every summer, monsoon rains in the highlands of Ethiopia swelled the river. In Egypt, it flooded the valley for about three months, and when the water pulled back,

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:50,000
it left a layer of black, fertile mud. Egyptians named their country after it: Kemet, the Black Land. The desert on both sides was Deshret, the Red Land.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The Egyptian year was even built around the river. It had three seasons: Flood, Growing and Harvest. Farming, taxes and festivals all followed that cycle.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,000
That mud gave Egypt something rare in the ancient world: a reliable surplus. Farmers grew far more grain than they needed, and that extra grain could feed builders, soldiers, priests and officials.

8
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:20,000
It paid for everything you're about to see. They measured the flood on stone stairways called Nilometers. A high flood meant big harvests and higher taxes.

9
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,000
A low one meant famine. Keep that in mind: everything in this story rises and falls with the river. The river was also Egypt's highway.

10
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The current carried boats north, and the wind usually blows from the north, so boats could sail back south. Moving grain, stone and armies along the valley was easy.

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:50,000
And the deserts on either side worked like walls. For centuries, they kept large armies out, so Egypt could grow rich without constantly defending itself.

12
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Around 3100 BC, a king from the south conquered the north and united Upper and Lower Egypt. Upper Egypt was the south, because the Nile flows north.

13
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Egyptians remembered the founder as Menes; historians link him to a king called Narmer. The capital, Memphis, sat where the valley meets the delta,

14
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:20,000
so it could control both. Around the same time, hieroglyphic writing appeared. One of its first big jobs was running the state: recording harvests,

15
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:30,000
taxes and stores, so one king could govern a country hundreds of kilometres long. Below the king, a chief minister, the vizier,

16
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:40,000
ran the government, and the country was divided into about 40 provinces, each with its own governor. Remember those governors.

17
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Egyptians worshipped many gods tied to the world around them: Ra, the sun; Osiris, god of the dead and of rebirth, linked to the flood's yearly return;

18
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,000
and his wife, Isis. These gods would last for 3,000 years. The king wasn't just a ruler. Egyptians believed he was a god on earth, whose job was to keep Ma'at,

19
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:10,000
the order of the universe, so the sun rose and the river flooded on time. That made him all-powerful, as long as the river behaved.

20
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:20,000
Egyptians believed the king's spirit lived on after death, and that his afterlife helped keep the whole country in order. So his tomb mattered to everyone.

21
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:30,000
Early kings were buried under flat mudbrick tombs called mastabas. Around 2650 BC, King Djoser's official Imhotep built his tomb in stone instead,

22
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:40,000
stacking six layers about 62 metres high. The Step Pyramid is the oldest large cut-stone building in the world, and it started the age of pyramids.

23
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:50,000
The next king, Sneferu, struggled to get the shape right. His Bent Pyramid changes angle halfway up, most likely because the first slope was too steep.

24
00:03:50,000 --> 00:04:00,000
His Red Pyramid finally got it right: the first true smooth-sided pyramid. His son Khufu built the Great Pyramid of Giza around 2560 BC:

25
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:10,000
about 2.3 million stone blocks, 146 metres tall when finished. Nothing built by humans stood taller for about 3,800 years.

26
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Deep inside, narrow passages lead to the king's granite burial chamber. By the time explorers reached it, it was empty. The river made it possible.

27
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:30,000
Most of the stone was cut right at Giza, but fine white limestone for the outer casing came by boat from quarries across the river, and huge granite beams for the king's burial chamber came from Aswan,

28
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:40,000
hundreds of kilometres upstream. How they raised the blocks is still debated. The most likely answer is ramps of mudbrick and rubble, with blocks dragged on wooden sledges.

29
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Wetting the sand in front of a sledge cuts the friction sharply, and an Egyptian tomb painting shows a worker doing exactly that. It was not built by slaves.

30
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Archaeologists found the builders' town beside the pyramids, with bakeries, breweries and sleeping halls, and the workers' own tombs nearby.

31
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:10,000
Many were farmers working in rotating shifts, likely during the flood season, when their fields were under water anyway. We even have a logbook from one of the crews.

32
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:20,000
In 2013, archaeologists found the oldest inscribed papyri ever discovered, including the diary of an inspector named Merer.

33
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:30,000
It records his team ferrying limestone from the quarries at Tura to a harbour at Giza, trip after trip, in the reign of Khufu.

34
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:40,000
A pyramid wasn't just a tomb. Each came with temples, a causeway and farmland whose harvests paid priests to make offerings to the dead king forever.

35
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:50,000
Over generations, more and more of Egypt's land and grain went to supporting dead kings and their priests. Khufu's son Khafre built the second pyramid at Giza,

36
00:05:50,000 --> 00:06:00,000
and the Great Sphinx was most likely carved in his reign. The third, built by Menkaure, is far smaller. After Giza, pyramids kept shrinking. That tells you something.

37
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:10,000
The kings were spending less, while the officials, priests and provincial governors who ran the state were growing richer and more independent. Late in this era,

38
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:20,000
kings began carving spells on their burial chamber walls to help them rise to the sky after death. These Pyramid Texts are among the oldest religious writings in the world.

39
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:30,000
For about 500 years, Egypt's god-kings built mountains of stone. Then the river stopped cooperating. Around 2200 BC, the climate shifted and the floods shrank, year after year.

40
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Harvests failed and people starved. And a god-king who couldn't make the river rise no longer looked much like a god. Pepi II, the last great king of this era,

41
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:50,000
may have reigned for more than 60 years. By then, provincial governors had made their posts hereditary and were ruling like independent kings.

42
00:06:50,000 --> 00:07:00,000
After him, central power collapsed. For more than a century, there was no single Egypt. Rival dynasties ruled from the north and from Thebes in the south,

43
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:10,000
local strongmen fought each other, and famine hit village after village. Historians call it the First Intermediate Period. Something lasting came out of it, too.

44
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:20,000
With the kings weakened, wealthy local families began claiming an afterlife that had once belonged to the king, painting the old royal spells inside their own coffins.

45
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:30,000
Around 2050 BC, Mentuhotep II of Thebes defeated the northern dynasty and reunited the country. This began the Middle Kingdom.

46
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Its kings had learned from the collapse. They moved the capital north, near the Faiyum oasis, drained its marshes to grow more food, cut the power of the provincial governors,

47
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:50,000
and built a chain of massive forts along the Nile in Nubia to guard the south. For about 300 years, Egypt was stable again.

48
00:07:50,000 --> 00:08:00,000
It was also Egypt's classic age of literature. Stories and teachings written in this period were copied in schools for centuries afterwards.

49
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:10,000
But after about two centuries, the royal line weakened. Dozens of short-lived kings followed one another, and their grip on the north slipped.

50
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:20,000
Meanwhile, settlers from the Levant had been moving into the northern delta. As the Middle Kingdom weakened, their leaders took control of the north,

51
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:30,000
around 1650 BC, ruling from their capital, Avaris. Egyptians called them "rulers of foreign lands." We call them the Hyksos.

52
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:40,000
They had horse-drawn chariots, powerful composite bows and better bronze weapons, which Egypt had barely used before. For the first time,

53
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:50,000
foreigners ruled the land of the pharaohs, and the Egyptian kings in Thebes had to accept them. Yet the Hyksos didn't try to erase Egypt.

54
00:08:50,000 --> 00:09:00,000
They took pharaohs' titles, wrote their names in hieroglyphs, and worshipped Egyptian gods alongside their own. It's the first time we see a pattern that runs through this whole story:

55
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:10,000
foreign rulers turning themselves into Egyptian kings. The kings of Thebes fought back. Seqenenre died in battle, and his mummy's skull still shows the axe and spear wounds.

56
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:20,000
Thebes was squeezed from both sides, because the Hyksos were in contact with the kingdom of Kush to the south. The next Theban king, Kamose,

57
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:30,000
intercepted a Hyksos letter urging Kush to attack Thebes while his army was away. The Thebans adopted the enemy's chariots and bows,

58
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:40,000
and around 1550 BC, Ahmose captured Avaris, drove the Hyksos out, and chased them into Canaan. Foreign rule changed Egypt.

59
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:50,000
It now had a professional army, and it had learned that the deserts were no longer enough. From now on, it would push its borders outward and build an empire.

60
00:09:50,000 --> 00:10:00,000
Ahmose's victory began the New Kingdom, Egypt's richest era. Thebes became its religious heart, and its god Amun, merged with the sun god Ra,

61
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:10,000
became king of the gods. As the empire grew, his temple at Karnak received a large share of the plunder, and his priests became enormously rich.

62
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:20,000
The new pharaohs went on the attack. Thutmose I led his army all the way to the Euphrates, in Syria, and deep into Nubia in the south.

63
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Almost every pyramid had been robbed by now, so the new pharaohs hid their tombs instead, cutting them deep into a desert valley near Thebes: the Valley of the Kings.

64
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:40,000
Around 1479 BC, the child Thutmose III became king, and his aunt and stepmother, Hatshepsut, ruled as regent. Within a few years,

65
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:50,000
she declared herself pharaoh, and her official images showed her with a king's headdress and the ceremonial false beard. To justify her rule,

66
00:10:50,000 --> 00:11:00,000
she had her temple walls carved with scenes showing the god Amun as her real father. She ruled for about 20 years, mostly through trade and building rather than war.

67
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:10,000
Her expedition to Punt, on Africa's Red Sea coast, brought back incense, ebony and living myrrh trees, and her terraced temple at Deir el-Bahari still stands.

68
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:20,000
She also raised giant granite obelisks at Karnak. Years after her death, her statues were smashed and her name was chiselled off monuments,

69
00:11:20,000 --> 00:11:30,000
most likely on Thutmose III's orders. Later official king lists left her out entirely. Once he ruled alone, Thutmose III became Egypt's greatest conqueror.

70
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:40,000
Around 1457 BC, rebel kings gathered at Megiddo, in what is now northern Israel. Thutmose took the narrowest of three routes,

71
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:50,000
a pass so tight his army marched in single file, and caught them by surprise. After a seven-month siege, Megiddo fell.

72
00:11:50,000 --> 00:12:00,000
We know these details because Thutmose had his campaigns carved on the walls of Karnak, listing battles, captured cities and tribute, much of it given to Amun's temple.

73
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:10,000
Over about 17 campaigns, he built the largest empire Egypt ever had, stretching from Syria to deep into Sudan, with Nubia's gold mines under Egyptian control.

74
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:20,000
He also found a way to hold it. Sons of conquered rulers were brought to Egypt and raised at court. When they went home to rule, they had grown up Egyptian, and loyal.

75
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:30,000
By the reign of Amenhotep III, Egypt was a superpower, rich enough to spend almost four decades building rather than fighting.

76
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:40,000
Clay tablets later found at Amarna preserve letters from the kings of Babylon and other great powers, writing to the pharaoh as equals and asking him for gold.

77
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:50,000
Then his son broke the system. Amenhotep IV pushed aside Egypt's many gods for one, the Aten, the disc of the sun, and renamed himself Akhenaten.

78
00:12:50,000 --> 00:13:00,000
Many historians see this partly as a move against the rich and powerful priests of Amun. He built a new capital, now called Amarna,

79
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:10,000
closed the old temples, and had Amun's name hacked off monuments. Only the king and his queen, Nefertiti, were shown worshipping the Aten directly.

80
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:20,000
Everyone else was meant to worship through them. Art changed too. The royal family was shown with long faces and rounded bodies,

81
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:30,000
in relaxed family scenes, a sharp break from 1,500 years of stiff, idealised royal images. Meanwhile, the empire drifted.

82
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Among the Amarna letters are pleas from Egypt's vassal rulers in Canaan, begging the pharaoh for troops against their enemies. Some seem to have gone unanswered.

83
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:50,000
His revolution didn't survive him. After his death, Amarna was abandoned, and Egypt went back to its old gods. The boy who soon took the throne, Tutankhaten, was about nine.

84
00:13:50,000 --> 00:14:00,000
His advisers reopened the old temples and changed his name to honour Amun: Tutankhamun. He died at around 19, and the exact cause is still debated.

85
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:10,000
A few years later, the army commander Horemheb became pharaoh. He tore down Akhenaten's temples, reused their blocks, and wiped the Amarna kings,

86
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:20,000
Tutankhamun included, from the official record. That's partly why Tutankhamun was forgotten. He had been buried in a small, hastily prepared tomb,

87
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:30,000
and later, workers digging a nearby tomb piled their debris over its entrance. Unlike almost every other royal tomb, it was never emptied by robbers.

88
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:40,000
In November 1922, British archaeologist Howard Carter uncovered its steps and sealed doorway. When his sponsor,

89
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:50,000
Lord Carnarvon, asked if he could see anything inside, Carter answered: "Yes, wonderful things." Inside were more than 5,000 objects.

90
00:14:50,000 --> 00:15:00,000
The king lay in three nested coffins, the innermost made of solid gold, about 110 kilograms, with the famous gold mask over his face.

91
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:10,000
And this was the burial of a minor king, which tells you how much was lost from the others. The discovery made Tutankhamun the most famous pharaoh in the world.

92
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:20,000
It came in the same year Egypt won formal independence from Britain, and Egyptians embraced the boy king as a symbol of their nation.

93
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:30,000
The treasures stayed in Egypt. Horemheb had no heir, so he passed the throne to another general, Ramesses I. His family, the Ramessides,

94
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:40,000
were soldiers from the delta, and they set out to rebuild Egypt's empire. The most famous was Ramesses II, who reigned for 66 years.

95
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:50,000
He built a new capital, Pi-Ramesses, in the eastern delta, closer to the frontier in Asia, and filled Egypt with temples and giant statues of himself,

96
00:15:50,000 --> 00:16:00,000
including the four colossal figures at Abu Simbel. Later Egyptians remembered him as the greatest king of all, and nine later pharaohs took his name.

97
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:10,000
In 1274 BC, he fought the Hittite empire for control of Syria at Kadesh, in one of the largest chariot battles in history.

98
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:20,000
Ramesses was caught in an ambush and had to fight his way out. The battle ended without a clear winner, though Ramesses advertised it at home as a great victory.

99
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:30,000
About 15 years later, the two empires signed one of the earliest peace treaties whose text survives, promising to help each other against attackers.

100
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:40,000
Ramesses later married a Hittite princess to seal the peace. Around 1200 BC, the Bronze Age world collapsed.

101
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:50,000
The Hittite empire fell, and cities across the eastern Mediterranean were destroyed. Under Ramesses III, Egypt beat back invaders it called the Sea Peoples,

102
00:16:50,000 --> 00:17:00,000
but war, failing trade and poor harvests drained it. Egypt soon lost its lands in Canaan and Syria. The state began to fail at home. Around 1157 BC,

103
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:10,000
royal tomb builders went unpaid and staged the first recorded strike in history. Within decades, officials were putting gangs on trial for robbing the royal tombs in the Valley of the Kings.

104
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:20,000
To save what was left, priests gathered the mummies of great pharaohs and hid them together in secret caches. That's why we can still see the faces of kings like Ramesses II today.

105
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:30,000
By about 1070 BC, the New Kingdom was over. The high priests of Amun ruled the south from Thebes, while separate kings ruled the delta.

106
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:40,000
Power slipped to outsiders. Families of Libyan descent, settled in Egypt for generations, took the throne. Then kings from Kush, in today's Sudan, marched north.

107
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:50,000
Around 727 BC, King Piye conquered Egypt, and his family ruled as pharaohs for more than half a century,

108
00:17:50,000 --> 00:18:00,000
restoring old temples and traditions. Next came the Assyrian empire. In 671 BC, its army took Memphis,

109
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:10,000
and in 663 BC it sacked Thebes and drove the Kushite kings back south for good. A delta prince, Psamtik I, used the Assyrians' retreat and hired Greek soldiers to reunite Egypt.

110
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:20,000
His dynasty was the last great native revival, and it lasted about 140 years. It opened Egypt to Greek traders,

111
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:30,000
and Psamtik's son Necho II tried to win back Syria. But in 605 BC, the Babylonian prince Nebuchadnezzar crushed the Egyptian army at Carchemish.

112
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:40,000
Egypt's days as an empire were over. In 525 BC, the Persian king Cambyses defeated the last king of that dynasty at Pelusium,

113
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:50,000
on Egypt's eastern border. Egypt became a province of the largest empire the world had yet seen, ruled from Iran.

114
00:18:50,000 --> 00:19:00,000
Some Persian kings, like Darius I, respected Egyptian temples and ruled through local officials. Others were harsher, and Egyptians rebelled again and again.

115
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:10,000
In 404 BC, they finally broke free. Independence lasted about 60 years. In 343 BC, Persia invaded again and won.

116
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:20,000
The pharaoh who lost that war, Nectanebo II, is usually counted as the last native Egyptian to rule Egypt for about 2,300 years.

117
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:30,000
That's the number from the start of this video. In 332 BC, Alexander the Great took Egypt from the Persians, and many Egyptians welcomed him.

118
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:40,000
He travelled to the oracle of Amun at Siwa, in the western desert, and was hailed as the god's son. Egypt accepted him as pharaoh.

119
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:50,000
He founded a new city on the coast: Alexandria. He left after a few months and never returned alive, but his body was later brought back and buried there.

120
00:19:50,000 --> 00:20:00,000
After Alexander's death in 323 BC, his general Ptolemy took Egypt, and Ptolemy's family ruled it for nearly 300 years.

121
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:10,000
The system was simple: Greek-speaking officials at the top, Egyptian farmers at the bottom, and the Nile's harvest taxed to fund the court.

122
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:20,000
To Egyptians, the Ptolemies presented themselves as pharaohs. They built huge temples in the traditional style, like those at Edfu and Dendera,

123
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:30,000
with their Greek names carved in hieroglyphs. Foreign rulers kept becoming, at least on temple walls, Egyptian kings. Alexandria became the greatest city of the Greek world,

124
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:40,000
with its lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders, and its Great Library. Scholars there worked out the size of the Earth and wrote the geometry still taught today. But the royal court itself spoke Greek.

125
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:50,000
The Ptolemies kept power in the family, often by brothers marrying sisters. Heavy taxes bred resentment, and for about 20 years, rebels held much of southern Egypt.

126
00:20:50,000 --> 00:21:00,000
In 196 BC, during that revolt, priests at Memphis issued a decree honouring the young king Ptolemy V. It was carved in three scripts: hieroglyphs,

127
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:10,000
the everyday Egyptian script called Demotic, and Greek. Remember this stone. The last ruler of the dynasty was Cleopatra VII. According to the ancient writer Plutarch,

128
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:20,000
she was the first of her family to learn Egyptian. She needed powerful allies to keep Egypt free of Rome, which was swallowing the Mediterranean. In 48 BC,

129
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:30,000
she was fighting her own brother for the throne when Julius Caesar arrived in Alexandria. She won his support, he defeated her rivals, and she had a son with him, Caesarion.

130
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:40,000
After Caesar's murder, she allied with Mark Antony, who controlled Rome's eastern provinces. That made them enemies of Caesar's heir, Octavian.

131
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:50,000
When Antony and Cleopatra lost the naval battle of Actium to Octavian in 31 BC, it was over. Antony killed himself,

132
00:21:50,000 --> 00:22:00,000
and in 30 BC Cleopatra died too, by tradition from a snake bite. Egypt became part of the Roman Empire. Octavian, now Emperor Augustus, kept Egypt as his personal
